<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vichy</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vichy is a spa town on the Allier in the Auvergne, central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, known since Roman times for its mineral springs. After the defeat of 1940 the French government settled there because of its many hotels, and parliament, meeting in its casino, voted full powers to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippe-petain">Philippe Pétain</a> on 10 July 1940, so that his collaborationist regime of 1940–1944 became known as Vichy France.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vichy.jpg" alt="Vichy" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thomas Kowalski · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2023-07-23_-_vue_sur_Vichy.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>During the Second World War Admiral Jean Decoux governed for <b>Vichy</b> France from 1940 to 1945, promoting youth and sports movements and local patriotism loyal to Marshal Pétain while sharing the colony with Japanese forces.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-indochina">French Indochina · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In occupied Europe Germany ruled through annexation, Reich commissariats in Norway, the Netherlands and the Soviet territories, military administrations, and collaborating regimes such as <b>Vichy</b> France and the Independent State of Croatia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/nazi-germany">Nazi Germany · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>A treaty of independence negotiated in 1936 under President Hashim al-Atassi was never ratified by France; British and Free French forces took the country from <b>Vichy</b> in July 1941, and French troops left in April 1946.</blockquote><a href="/empire/syrian-arab-republic">Syrian Arab Republic · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Pétain’s <b>Vichy</b> regime of 1940–1944 combined authoritarian conservatism with heightened antisemitism.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/conservatism">Conservatism · In Power</a></li><li><blockquote>Fascist movements elsewhere took up the tune in the 1930s and 1940s: the British Union of Fascists, the Spanish Falange, the Legion of French Volunteers Against Bolshevism in <b>Vichy</b> France, the All-Russian Fascist Organisation of émigrés in the United States and Finland's Lapua Movement and Patriotic People's Movement.</blockquote><a href="/songs/horst-wessel-lied">Horst-Wessel-Lied</a></li><li><blockquote>&quot;Maréchal, nous voilà !&quot; (&quot;Marshal, here we are!&quot;) is a French propaganda song of 1941 in honour of Marshal Philippe Pétain, head of the <b>Vichy</b> regime, an authoritarian and antisemitic state set up during the German occupation.</blockquote><a href="/songs/marechal-nous-voila">Maréchal, nous voilà !</a></li></ul></section>
